  [38;5;153mmember[39m. Owners manage membership and can archive; members do everything else. I'd resist a richer role system until
  something concrete demands it — role hierarchies are easy to add and painful to simplify.

  [1m[38;5;153mtasks[0m — [38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153mproject_id[39m, [38;5;153mtitle[39m, [38;5;153mdescription[39m (nullable), [38;5;153mstatus[39m, [38;5;153massignee_id[39m (nullable), [38;5;153mdue_date[39m (nullable),
  [38;5;153mcreated_by[39m, [38;5;153mcreated_at[39m, [38;5;153mupdated_at[39m.

  [38;5;153mstatus[39m as an enum: [38;5;153mtodo[39m, [38;5;153min_progress[39m, [38;5;153mdone[39m. Three states because they're the ones that change behavior. I deliberately
  left out priority, labels, subtasks, attachments, and comments — each is a real feature, none is needed to have a
  working shared task list, and every one of them adds surface to the notification model later.

  [1mDeletion.[0m Soft-delete tasks ([38;5;153mdeleted_at[39m) rather than hard-delete. Two reasons: it makes "deleted your task" a
  notifiable event later rather than a dangling reference, and it makes accidental deletion recoverable. Projects
  archive rather than delete.

  [1mWhere the later projects attach.[0m Project #3 adds a [38;5;153mtask_events[39m table — [38;5;153mtask_id[39m, [38;5;153mactor_id[39m, [38;5;153mevent_type[39m, [38;5;153mchanged_fields[39m
  (JSONB), [38;5;153mcreated_at[39m — written by the service layer inside the same transaction as the mutation. Project #4 then adds
  [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m and [38;5;153mnotifications[39m reading from it. I'm flagging this so the core's service layer is shaped to
  accommodate it, [1mnot[0m proposing we build either now.

  [1mOne thing I want to name as an open assumption:[0m I've assumed tasks are flat — no parent/child, no dependencies. If
  you're picturing hierarchy, say so now. It's the one item on this list that's genuinely expensive to retrofit, since
  it changes queries, permissions, and the meaning of "changed" for notifications.
